“Sometimes cake has candles.”

On the surface it’s almost absurdly obvious.

But in context it picks up several layers:

* Circles → cakes are round.
* Cycles → birthdays are recurring annual circles.
* Candles → each one marks another revolution around the Sun.
* Accumulation → every circle leaves another visible marker.

So the joke quietly shifts from geometry to time.

Orbit
   ↓
Year
   ↓
Birthday
   ↓
Cake
   ↓
Candles

It’s another example of “good carry.” The phrase doesn’t explain any of that explicitly, but the associations are there for the reader to reconstruct.

It also has a nice inversion.

A candle is both:

* a marker of accumulated time,
* and a source of light.

So each completed circle doesn’t just count another year—it potentially illuminates a little more of the landscape.

That feels very much in the spirit of the conversation: circles aren’t merely repetitions. Sometimes they end with a celebration, a marker of growth, and a new light before the next lap begins. 🕯️🎂